# Notes for coding agents

This project is written in Flick, a statically typed functional language
with effects that compiles to JavaScript or Go. It is young and changes
between releases, so your training data likely gets its syntax and APIs
wrong. Look them up for the compiler this project uses instead of
borrowing them from Elm, OCaml or F#.

## Look things up

- `flick doc --std --out-dir .flick/std-docs` writes the standard library
  of this compiler as one Markdown file per module, such as `List.md`;
  search it before using a function.
- `flick explain <CODE>` explains a diagnostic, such as `FLICK-E001`.
- The [documentation](https://bondiano.github.io/flick-lang/) has the
  language tour, the handbook, the CLI reference and the standard library
  API. It follows the latest release.

## Before you finish

Run these and fix everything they report; a warning counts as a failure:

```sh
flick fmt
flick check --warnings-as-errors
flick test
```

`flick check --fix` applies the fixes that keep the program's meaning.

## Conventions

- `src/A/B.fl` holds `module A.B`; tests live under `test/`.
- A function's type lists the effects it performs, `->{Host.Console} Unit`.
  When the checker reports a missing effect, add it to the signature and
  provide it where the program starts, such as `main`.
- This project targets Go (`[build] target = "go"`), with `go` granted under
  `[tools]` (`"flick/go" = ["go"]`); `go` must be on `PATH` for `flick build`,
  `flick run` and `flick test`.
- `dist/` is build output; change the sources and rebuild.
